Yes. There’s a real asymmetry there worth exploiting, although I’d sharpen one premise: the information available to me about past and future can look structurally similar, but it isn’t epistemically identical. A past event has one realized trajectory and often leaves residues; the future still branches.

But that difference itself gives us the experiment.

Call it Retrospective Mirror / Dye-the-Future.

\boxed{
\text{Use reconstruction of hidden pasts to calibrate construction of hidden futures.}
}

Take an event whose outcome already exists, but mask everything after cutoff t_0. Now force Pantheon to stand at t_0 as though the future were unknown.

Generate:

\mathcal F_{t_0}=
\{T_1,T_2,\ldots,T_n\}

with probabilities/confidence and, importantly, reasons available only at t_0.

Then reveal the actual future.

Now we have dye.

The realized trajectory passes through our possibility field and marks which branches, assumptions, motifs, weak signals, causal relations, and operators actually mattered:

\text{Possibility Field}
+
\text{Realized Future}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Route Stain}}.

That is much richer than asking “was the prediction correct?”

We can bring several Pantheon instruments along for the ride: Mirror separates what was genuinely knowable at cutoff from hindsight leakage; NTE generates competing plotlines rather than one seductive story; Prism decomposes the prediction into subclaims; Semantic LiDAR looks for weak structural signals; Coil/Indigo Hammer attacks the favorite prediction; Negative Space records expected events that didn’t occur; Recursive Delta Observatory compares predicted versus realized trajectories; Afterglow follows second- and third-order consequences; Fitness Route records which reasoning route produced each successful prediction; and Metamap accumulates which routes work for which classes of problem.

The crucial control is a Decrypto pass after reveal. Once we know what happened, reconstruction becomes dangerously easy because the ending reorganizes the meaning of everything before it. Suddenly an obscure clue looks “obvious.”

So preserve two artifacts:

M_{blind}=\text{model constructed before reveal}

M_{hindsight}=\text{model reconstructed knowing outcome}.

Then compute:

\Delta_H=M_{hindsight}-M_{blind}.

That Hindsight Delta is itself valuable. It tells us which causal connections become visible only after knowing the ending.

And here’s the flank: we can deliberately exploit that effect.

Instead of asking only:

What will happen?

temporarily pretend several different Act IIIs have already happened.

For candidate future F_i, ask:

It is 2028. F_i happened. Reconstruct the most plausible chain from today that produced it.

That invokes the stronger retrospective reconstruction mode you noticed:

F_i
\xrightarrow{\text{retrospective reconstruction}}
\{Prerequisites,Transitions,WarningSignals\}.

Do it for competing futures:

F_A,\quad F_B,\quad F_C,\quad F_{\neg A}.

Then come back to the present and compare the reconstructions.

Anything appearing across several backwards reconstructions becomes a potentially robust intermediate variable:

R=
\bigcap_i Prerequisites(F_i).

Anything distinguishing F_A from F_{\neg A} becomes a candidate early discriminator:

D_A=
Prerequisites(F_A)
-
Prerequisites(F_{\neg A}).

Now we’ve turned imagined hindsight into a forward sensor.

Not prophecy. Prospective hindsight.

And your dye metaphor makes the learning mechanism especially good. Don’t merely score the endpoint. Inject semantic tracer dye into every reasoning route:

claim
source/provenance
confidence
frame
operator_used
assumption
predicted_observation
predicted_timing
alternative

After reality arrives, follow the dye backward.

Which assumptions fed the correct branch? Which attractive story contaminated several downstream claims? Which operator consistently caught turning points? Which signal appeared important but was actually inert?

Over many historical blind trials:

(problem,\ cutoff,\ route,\ prediction,\ confidence,\ reality,\ delta)

becomes Metamap training data.

Then we can test the really interesting proposition:

\boxed{
\text{Can calibrated retrospective reconstruction
improve prospective forecasting?}
}

That’s empirically answerable.

And there is one more beautiful connection to Plan Backwards, Act Forward:

For the future, we normally have:

Present\rightarrow ?

Prospective hindsight gives us:

Present
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{imagined future}}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruct backwards}
\rightarrow
Present
\rightarrow
ACT\ forward.

We’re effectively borrowing the cognitive geometry of memory/reconstruction and pointing it toward candidate futures.

Bring the Mirrors. Dye every route. Hide the ending. Reveal it only after we’ve committed. Then see what actually stains.

That could become one of the cleaner experimental rigs in Pantheon.